KNOWNBUG libstdcxx
main.cpp
--cpp11 --object-bits 12 --unwind 8 --no-unwinding-assertions
^\[main\.assertion\.1\] line \d+ the thread ran before join returned: SUCCESS$
^\[main\.assertion\.2\] line \d+ WRONG \(must FAIL\): FAILURE$
^\*\* 1 of \d+ failed
^VERIFICATION FAILED$
^EXIT=10$
^SIGNAL=0$
--
^warning: ignoring
^CONVERSION ERROR$
--
libstdc++ defines std::thread::_M_start_thread (which starts the thread)
and join in its compiled library (thread.cc), and libc++ join and detach
(thread.cpp); cpp_typecheck_stdlib.cpp now models them with CBMC's
pthread_create and pthread_join.  Symbolic execution then stops with
"pointer handling for concurrency is unsound" (goto_symex_state.cpp,
issue #305) before any property is checked: in the new thread, the
destructor of the thread's state writes its `this', a local whose address
is taken (so taken to be shared), and symex does not track pointers shared
between threads.  cpp11_std_thread_starts_a_thread checks that the thread
is started.  Fixing #305 makes this test pass.
